entry.content:
&lt;h2 id=&quot;motivation&quot;&gt;Motivation&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In an interview, I was once asked to explain the compilation steps, and my mind went blank. I still can&#x27;t say why. Maybe I didn&#x27;t know the steps. Maybe I knew them, but only on the surface. Or maybe I was just nervous. I left the room without knowing which one it was, and that bothered me more than the question did.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Most of the time, compiling is one command and a program appears. It works, so it&#x27;s easy to never look inside. That interview showed me that using a tool and understanding it are not the same thing.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So I decided to settle it. In this post I go through each stage myself, run it, and look at what comes out. If I can explain every step in my own words and show its output, Its understood completely then.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;why-compile-at-all&quot;&gt;Why compile at all?&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Here is a small C program. Anyone can read it.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F9E2AF;&quot;&gt;#include&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;lt;stdio.h&amp;gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;int&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt; main&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;void&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;{&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    int&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj1 &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 1&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    int&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj2 &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;    printf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;%d&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F5C2E7;&quot;&gt; \n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj1 &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;+&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    return&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;}&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A CPU can&#x27;t. It only runs machine instructions, which are just numbers in binary. So the .c file has to be turned into those instructions before anything can happen, and that translation is what compilation is.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;the-four-stages&quot;&gt;The four stages&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When I type gcc main.c, it feels like one step, but it is really four programs running one after another, each handing a file to the next.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;plain&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;main.c  -&amp;gt;  main.i  -&amp;gt;  main.s  -&amp;gt;  main.o  -&amp;gt;  main&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        gcc -E      gcc -S      gcc -c      gcc&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Preprocessing, compilation, assembly and linking. I&#x27;ll go through them in order. All the output below comes from GCC 13.3 on x86-64 Linux, so on another machine, say an ARM microcontroller, the instructions will look different but the stages are the same.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To give the preprocessor something to do, I added a macro and a comment to the program.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F9E2AF;&quot;&gt;#include&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;lt;stdio.h&amp;gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F9E2AF;&quot;&gt;#define&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt; GREETING_NUM&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 1&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;int&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt; main&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;void&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;{&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    int&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj1 &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; GREETING_NUM&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt; &#x2F;* first value *&#x2F;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    int&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj2 &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;    printf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;%d&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F5C2E7;&quot;&gt; \n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj1 &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;+&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    return&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;}&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;&lt;h2 id=&quot;preprocessing&quot;&gt;Preprocessing&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The preprocessor works on text only. It doesn&#x27;t understand C yet. It just looks for lines starting with #, like #include, #define and #ifdef, and acts on them. An #include is replaced by the whole contents of that header, a #define macro is replaced wherever it&#x27;s used, code inside #if and #ifdef blocks is kept or thrown away depending on the condition, and comments are removed.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;shellscript&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;gcc&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; -E main.c -o main.i&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The result is still plain C. Mine came out at 825 lines, almost all of it from stdio.h being pasted in. The part I care about is at the bottom.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;# &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;5&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;quot;main.c&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;int&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt; main&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;void&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;{&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    int&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj1 &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 1&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    int&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj2 &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;    printf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;%d&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F5C2E7;&quot;&gt; \n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj1 &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;+&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; obj2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    return&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;}&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GREETING_NUM has become 1 and the comment is gone. The line starting with # 5 is a marker that tells the compiler which file and line the code came from, which is how error messages can point back to your original source.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;compilation&quot;&gt;Compilation&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is the stage the word usually refers to. The compiler reads the preprocessed code, checks the syntax and the types, optimizes it, and writes assembly for the target CPU.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;shellscript&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;gcc&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; -S main.i -o main.s&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While writing this I noticed I had typed onj1 instead of obj1 in my notes. That&#x27;s the kind of mistake this stage catches. The compiler stops with an error about an undeclared name and never produces assembly. (Errors about a function that&#x27;s declared but never defined show up later, at linking.)&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Here is main.s, with no optimization.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;asm&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;.&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;LC0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        .string &amp;quot;%d \n&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;main&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        pushq   %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rbp&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;        movq&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;    %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rsp&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rbp&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        subq    &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;$16&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rsp&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        movl    &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;$1&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, -&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;8&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(%&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rbp&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;)       &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt; # obj1 = 1&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        movl    &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;$2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, -&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;4&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(%&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rbp&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;)       &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt; # obj2 = 2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        movl    -&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;8&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(%&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rbp&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;), %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;edx&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        movl    -&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;4&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;(%&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rbp&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;), %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;eax&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        addl    %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;edx&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;eax&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;          # obj1 + obj2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        movl    %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;eax&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;esi&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;          # second argument to printf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        leaq    .LC0(%&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rip&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;), %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rax&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;    # address of the format string&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;        movq&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;    %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rax&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;rdi&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;          # first argument to printf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        movl    &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;$0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;eax&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;        call&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;    printf@PLT&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        movl    &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;$0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, %&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F38BA8;&quot;&gt;eax&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;            # return 0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;        leave&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;        ret&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I trimmed the directives at the top, but the rest is untouched, and you can follow the C through it. The two variables are stored on the stack, they are added, and the result and the address of the string are put into registers for the call to printf. The return value goes into eax.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One thing to hold on to is that printf is only called here. The compiler has no idea where its code lives.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;assembly&quot;&gt;Assembly&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The assembler turns this text into actual bytes and writes an object file, main.o on Linux and similar systems, or main.obj with some other toolchains.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;shellscript&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;gcc&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; -c main.s -o main.o&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An object file isn&#x27;t readable, so I used objdump -d -M intel main.o to see the bytes next to the instructions.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;plain&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;0000000000000000 &amp;lt;main&amp;gt;:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;   0:   55                      push   rbp&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;   1:   48 89 e5                mov    rbp,rsp&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;   4:   48 83 ec 10             sub    rsp,0x10&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;   8:   c7 45 f8 01 00 00 00    mov    DWORD PTR [rbp-0x8],0x1&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;   f:   c7 45 fc 02 00 00 00    mov    DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4],0x2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  16:   8b 55 f8                mov    edx,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x8]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  19:   8b 45 fc                mov    eax,DWORD PTR [rbp-0x4]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  1c:   01 d0                   add    eax,edx&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  1e:   89 c6                   mov    esi,eax&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  20:   48 8d 05 00 00 00 00    lea    rax,[rip+0x0]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  27:   48 89 c7                mov    rdi,rax&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  2a:   b8 00 00 00 00          mov    eax,0x0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  2f:   e8 00 00 00 00          call   34 &amp;lt;main+0x34&amp;gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  34:   b8 00 00 00 00          mov    eax,0x0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  39:   c9                      leave&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  3a:   c3                      ret&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The middle column is the machine code. 55 is push rbp and c3 is ret. This is what the CPU actually runs.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What surprised me is the lea and call lines, where the address part is all zeros. The assembler doesn&#x27;t know where the string or printf will end up, so it leaves a blank and writes a note for later. nm and objdump -r show that.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;plain&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;$ nm main.o&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;0000000000000000 T main&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;                 U printf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;$ objdump -r main.o&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;OFFSET           TYPE              VALUE&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;0000000000000023 R_X86_64_PC32     .rodata-0x0000000000000004&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;0000000000000030 R_X86_64_PLT32    printf-0x0000000000000004&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;T means main is defined in this file. U means printf is undefined, so someone else has to supply it. The two relocation entries say that the bytes at offsets 0x23 and 0x30 need to be patched with real addresses later. So an object file has the machine code, the data, a table of symbols and these relocation notes, but it isn&#x27;t a complete program yet.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;linking&quot;&gt;Linking&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The linker takes the object files and libraries and joins them into one executable. It looks at that U printf, finds printf in the C library, fills in the blanks from the relocation notes, and lays everything out at real addresses.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;shellscript&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;gcc&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; main.o -o main&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Disassembling the final file shows the zeros are gone.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;plain&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;0000000000001139 &amp;lt;main&amp;gt;:&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    ...&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    1159:   48 8d 05 a4 0e 00 00    lea    rax,[rip+0xea4]        # 2004&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    ...&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    1168:   e8 c3 fe ff ff          call   1030 &amp;lt;printf@plt&amp;gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;main now sits at 0x1139, the string is at 0x2004, and the call points somewhere real. The @plt part is there because gcc links to the C library dynamically by default. The code for printf isn&#x27;t copied into my program. It&#x27;s loaded from libc when the program starts, and ldd main confirms that by listing libc.so.6.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For a fully self-contained file you can link statically with gcc -static main.o -o main. That&#x27;s the normal choice in bare-metal and embedded work, where there&#x27;s no operating system to load shared libraries.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And then it runs.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;plain&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;$ .&#x2F;main&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;3&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;&lt;h2 id=&quot;wrapping-up&quot;&gt;Wrapping up&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Plain gcc main.c does all four stages at once. If you want to see the files in between, gcc -save-temps main.c keeps them.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If someone asks me again, the short version is this. The preprocessor expands the text, the compiler turns it into assembly, the assembler turns that into an object file, and the linker joins object files and libraries into an executable.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;

&lt;h2 id=&quot;description&quot;&gt;Description&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Coming from Java&#x27;s String class to C&#x27;s character arrays taught me more about memory than any textbook. Here&#x27;s what I learned about string copy, the null terminator and why memmove exists.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;motivation&quot;&gt;Motivation&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I worked with strings in Java for a long time without ever thinking about them. They were immutable, garbage collected and safe to copy around. I never wondered where the characters lived or what really happened when I assigned one string to another.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;C changed that quickly. Copying a string meant deciding where the bytes go, how much room there is, and who is to blame when there isn&#x27;t enough. A single line of copying suddenly involved memory allocation, pointers and raw data all at once.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I wanted to slow down and understand it properly, so this post is me working through it. What does a string look like in memory? What is the zero at the end for? How does a copy actually work, and why do both memcpy and memmove exist?&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;a-string-is-just-bytes&quot;&gt;A string is just bytes&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In Java, a String is an object that knows its own length and won&#x27;t let you read past the end. In C, a string is an array of char that happens to end with a zero byte.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;char&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; s&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;[]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;quot;Hi&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I printed what is actually stored:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;plain&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;sizeof(s) = 3&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;strlen(s) = 2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;s[0] = 72   (&amp;#39;H&amp;#39;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;s[1] = 105  (&amp;#39;i&amp;#39;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;s[2] = 0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Three bytes for a two-letter word. The last byte is the terminator. Nothing in C stores the length of a string, so functions like strlen and printf just walk forward until they hit that zero.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This also changed how I think about comparing strings. In Java I knew == compared references and equals compared content. C has the same trap. Two arrays holding &quot;Hello&quot; are at different addresses, so a == b is false, while strcmp(a, b) returns 0 because the contents match.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;the-escape-character&quot;&gt;The &quot;escape character&quot;&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When I started writing this, I called that last byte the escape character. That&#x27;s wrong. The escape character is the backslash. It begins escape sequences like \n and \t. The sequence \0 is just a way of writing the byte with value zero, and that byte is the null terminator.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What does it mean? Simply &quot;the string ends here&quot;. And if it&#x27;s missing or overwritten, nothing stops the functions from reading on.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;char&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; x&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;[&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;8&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt; {&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;A&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;B&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;C&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F5C2E7;&quot;&gt;\0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;X&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;Y&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;Z&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F5C2E7;&quot;&gt;\0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;};&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;printf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;%s&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F5C2E7;&quot;&gt;\n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; x&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;     &#x2F;&#x2F; ABC&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;x&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;[&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;3&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;#39;-&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;printf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;%s&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F5C2E7;&quot;&gt;\n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; x&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;     &#x2F;&#x2F; ABC-XYZ&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The second printf just kept going and picked up the XYZ next to it. I was lucky that there was another zero at x[7]. If there hadn&#x27;t been one, it would have read whatever happened to sit after the array, which could be garbage or a crash.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;copying-a-string&quot;&gt;Copying a string&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Once you know about the terminator, copying a string is easy to picture. You copy the characters one at a time, and then you copy the zero at the end.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;while&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt; (&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;*&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;src &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;!=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F5C2E7;&quot;&gt;\0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;) {&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;    *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;dst &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;= *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;src&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    dst&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;++&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    src&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;++&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;}&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;*&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;dst &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F5C2E7;&quot;&gt;\0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For &quot;Hi&quot;, this copies &#x27;H&#x27;, then &#x27;i&#x27;, then sees the zero in the source and stops. The last line writes a zero into the destination so it becomes a proper string too. The real strcpy does the same job, just written more compactly and tuned for speed.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The problem is what strcpy never checks. It looks at the source to know when to stop, but it has no idea how big the destination is.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;char&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; dst&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;[&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;4&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;];&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;strcpy&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;dst&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;quot;Hello&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;     &#x2F;&#x2F; needs 6 bytes, dst only has 4&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&quot;Hello&quot; needs six bytes including the zero. The array has four. strcpy happily writes the extra two bytes past the end, over whatever was stored next to it.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I assumed strncpy was the fix, since it takes a size limit. It does stop at the limit, but it has a trap of its own.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;char&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; dst&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;[&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;4&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;];&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;strncpy&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;dst&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;quot;Hello&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 4&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This copies H, e, l, l and stops because it reached the limit of four. There is no room left for the zero, and strncpy doesn&#x27;t add one. When I checked, the last byte was &#x27;l&#x27;. So dst isn&#x27;t a valid string anymore, and printing it would read on past the array looking for a zero.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The habit I settled on is snprintf.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;char&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; dst&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;[&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;4&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;];&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;snprintf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;dst&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; sizeof&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #EBA0AC;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt; dst&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;quot;%s&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;quot;Hello&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;     &#x2F;&#x2F; dst holds &amp;quot;Hel&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It never writes more than the size you give it, and it always leaves room for the zero. If the text is too long, it gets cut short instead of overflowing. Losing the end of a string is a much better problem to have than writing over memory that isn&#x27;t yours.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;memcpy&quot;&gt;memcpy&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;memcpy(dest, src, n) is different because it doesn&#x27;t care about zero bytes. It copies exactly n bytes, whatever they are. That makes it usable for structs, integer arrays and binary data, not only text. The price is that you must say how many bytes to copy.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I used to think memcpy was the slow, risky one, but that isn&#x27;t true. It&#x27;s usually very fast. The catch is a rule: the source and destination must not overlap. If they do, the behaviour is undefined.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;why-overlap-matters&quot;&gt;Why overlap matters&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Say I want to shift part of a buffer two places to the right, inside the same buffer.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;plain&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;index:   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;before:  A B C D E F G H&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I want to move the first six bytes so they start at index 2. If I copy from the front, one byte at a time:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;for&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt; (&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;int&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; i &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; i &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 6&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; i&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;++&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    buf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;[&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt;2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; +&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; i&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; buf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;[&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;i&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;];&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I get ABABABAB. The first write puts an A at index 2, which was a C I hadn&#x27;t copied yet. From then on I&#x27;m copying the damage forward.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If I copy from the back instead, I get ABABCDEF, which is right. But if I wanted to shift left, copying from the front would be the correct choice. So the right direction depends on how the two regions overlap.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;memmove&quot;&gt;memmove&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;memmove takes the same arguments as memcpy, but it is guaranteed to give the right answer even when the source and destination overlap. The trick is that it looks at the two addresses before it copies anything, and uses them to decide which end to start from.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Take the same buffer as before.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;plain&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;index:   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;before:  A B C D E F G H&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If the destination is after the source, I&#x27;m shifting right. The danger is writing over source bytes I haven&#x27;t read yet, and that happens at the front of the region, so memmove starts from the back instead. Moving the first six bytes to index 2, it copies F to index 7, then E to index 6, then D to index 5, and so on down to A. By the time a write lands on a byte that was part of the source, that byte has already been copied. The result is ABABCDEF.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If the destination is before the source, I&#x27;m shifting left, and the danger flips to the other end. So memmove starts from the front. Moving the last six bytes to index 0, it copies C to index 0, D to index 1, and so on. Each write only lands on a byte that has already been read. The result is CDEFGHGH.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If the two regions don&#x27;t overlap at all, either direction works.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Written out by hand, the whole idea is only a few lines.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;void&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;my_memmove&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;void&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #EBA0AC;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;dest&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt; const void&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #EBA0AC;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;src&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt; size_t&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #EBA0AC;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt; n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;{&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    unsigned char&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;d &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt; (&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;unsigned char&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;dest&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    const unsigned char&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;s &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt; (&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;const unsigned char&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;src&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    if&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt; (&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;n &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;==&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 0&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; ||&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; dest &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;==&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; src&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;        return&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; dest&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    if&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt; (&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;d &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; s &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; d &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; s &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;+&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;    {&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;        &#x2F;&#x2F; Copy backward when memory overlaps&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        d &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;+=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;        s &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;+=&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;        while&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt; (&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;--&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;            *--&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;d &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;= *--&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;s&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;    }&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    else&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;    {&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;        &#x2F;&#x2F; Copy forward&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;        while&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt; (&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;--&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;            *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;d&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;++ = *&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;s&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;++&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;    }&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;    return&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; dest&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;}&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The check d &amp;gt; s &amp;amp;&amp;amp; d &amp;lt; s + n is the overlap test. It asks whether the destination starts inside the source region. If it does, copying forward would overwrite source bytes I haven’t read yet, so I copy backward. In every other case, forward is safe.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The real memmove does the same thing, just faster because it copies many bytes at a time.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A common use is deleting from the middle of a string. To remove the C from &quot;ABCDEF&quot;, move everything after it one place to the left.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;c&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;char&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; s&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #CBA6F7;&quot;&gt;[]&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; =&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt; &amp;quot;ABCDEF&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;memmove&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;s &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;+&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 2&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; s &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;+&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 3&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt; strlen&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;s &lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt;+&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 3&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #94E2D5;&quot;&gt; +&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #FAB387;&quot;&gt; 1&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #89B4FA;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;printf&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;(&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;%s&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #F5C2E7;&quot;&gt;\n&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #A6E3A1;&quot;&gt;&amp;quot;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;,&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span&gt; s&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;&quot;&gt;);&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #9399B2;font-style: italic;&quot;&gt;                 &#x2F;&#x2F; ABDEF&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The plus one matters. memmove only moves bytes and knows nothing about strings, so the terminator has to be included in the count or the result won&#x27;t end properly.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;what-memmove-doesn-t-do&quot;&gt;What memmove doesn&#x27;t do&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It fixes overlap, not overflow. It will copy exactly n bytes and trust you that both buffers are big enough. It can&#x27;t check the destination size, and it can&#x27;t tell whether the source really has n readable bytes.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A few mistakes I want to avoid. Because n is unsigned, a subtraction that goes below zero wraps into a huge number. Copying strlen(s) bytes leaves out the terminator, so it needs to be strlen(s) + 1. And the size should come from sizeof or a length I track myself, checked before I copy.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;closing-thoughts&quot;&gt;Closing thoughts&lt;&#x2F;h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Java handled memory safety for me, and I never noticed. In C it&#x27;s all on me. I need to know where the data starts, where it ends and where it&#x27;s going. That was the real lesson from strings.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;

&lt;h3 id=&quot;what-is-machine-learning&quot;&gt;What is Machine Learning?&lt;&#x2F;h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Machine learning is the field of study that uses data and algorithms to build models that can make predictions or decisions without being explicitly programmed for each scenario.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In traditional programming, I write the rules and the computer applies them to data to produce answers. In machine learning, I give the computer the data and the answers, and it figures out the rules on its own.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;giallo&quot; style=&quot;color: #CDD6F4; background-color: #1E1E2E;&quot;&gt;&lt;code data-lang=&quot;plain&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Traditional programming:   Rules + Data    --&amp;gt;  Answers&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;giallo-l&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Machine learning:          Data + Answers  --&amp;gt;  Rules (the model)&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;span&gt;&lt;&#x2F;code&gt;&lt;&#x2F;pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For example, if we try writing rules to detect spam (Eg: &quot;if the email contains the word &lt;em&gt;lottery&lt;&#x2F;em&gt; then mark it as spam&quot;) it won&#x27;t work for long, because spammers change their wording and approach. With machine learning, I provide thousands of emails already labelled spam or not spam, and it would learn the patterns itself.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;how-does-it-work&quot;&gt;How does it work?&lt;&#x2F;h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A machine learning system identifies patterns in historical data and uses those patterns to make reliable predictions on new inputs.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The basic loop looks like this:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Collect data:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; For ex, house size and the prices they were sold for.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Train a model:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; The algorithm adjusts the model&#x27;s internal parameters so its predictions get closer to the known answers.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Evaluate:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; Test the model on data it has never seen.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Predict:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; Use the trained model on new inputs. This step is called &lt;em&gt;inference&lt;&#x2F;em&gt;.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A few terms that come up everywhere:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Features:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; the inputs the model looks at (house size, number of rooms).&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Label &#x2F; target:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; the answer we want the model to predict (price).&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Model:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; the function that maps features to a prediction.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Training:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; the process of tuning the model using data.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ul&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;types-of-machine-learning&quot;&gt;Types of machine learning&lt;&#x2F;h3&gt;
&lt;table&gt;&lt;thead&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Type&lt;&#x2F;th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Data it learns from&lt;&#x2F;th&gt;&lt;&#x2F;tr&gt;&lt;&#x2F;thead&gt;&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Supervised learning&lt;&#x2F;td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Inputs with known labels&lt;&#x2F;td&gt;&lt;&#x2F;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Unsupervised learning&lt;&#x2F;td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Inputs with no labels&lt;&#x2F;td&gt;&lt;&#x2F;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Reinforcement learning&lt;&#x2F;td&gt;&lt;td&gt;Rewards and penalties from trial and error&lt;&#x2F;td&gt;&lt;&#x2F;tr&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;tbody&gt;&lt;&#x2F;table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Supervised learning is where most people start, and it splits into two common problems:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Regression:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; predict a number (price, temperature).&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Classification:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; predict a category (spam or not spam, cat or dog).&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ul&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;how-do-we-know-we-are-on-the-right-path&quot;&gt;How do we know we are on the right path?&lt;&#x2F;h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We measure success by checking whether performance on a clearly defined task improves with experience. A common way to state this: a program learns from experience &lt;strong&gt;E&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; with respect to a task &lt;strong&gt;T&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; and a performance measure &lt;strong&gt;P&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;, if its performance on T, as measured by P, improves with E.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Take training a machine to play chess:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;a. &lt;strong&gt;Task (T):&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; Playing chess games, for example choosing moves that lead to a win.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;b. &lt;strong&gt;Performance (P):&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; How well it does, measured for example by its win rate against human players or other programs.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;c. &lt;strong&gt;Experience (E):&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; The games it learns from, such as historical games, board positions and outcomes, or games it plays against itself.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If the win rate keeps going up as the machine sees more games, it is learning.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;training-vs-testing&quot;&gt;Training vs. testing&lt;&#x2F;h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A model can score perfectly on data it has already seen simply by memorizing it. That tells us nothing about how it will behave on new data. So we split the data:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Training set:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; used to teach the model.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Test set:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; kept hidden until the end, used only to measure real performance.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This leads to the two classic problems:&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Underfitting:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; the model is too simple and performs poorly even on the training data.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Overfitting:&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt; the model memorizes the training data, including its noise, and performs poorly on new data.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The goal is a model that &lt;strong&gt;generalizes&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;: it does well on data it has never seen.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;summary&quot;&gt;Summary&lt;&#x2F;h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Machine learning builds models that learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Training tunes the model on known examples. And we get the model prediction on new untrained data(Inference).&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Progress is measured with a defined task(T), a performance measure(P), and experience(E).&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;We always evaluate the model on unseen data that hasn&#x27;t been trained on, to catch overfitting.&lt;&#x2F;li&gt;
&lt;&#x2F;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Next up: &lt;strong&gt;Linear Regression&lt;&#x2F;strong&gt;, the simplest supervised learning algorithm, and a good first model to build.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
